<figure class="snapshot" data-export-snapshot aria-labelledby="r07-snap-title">
 <figcaption id="r07-snap-title"><span>Figure 2</span> Which view supplies the next batch?</figcaption>
 <div class="choice" role="group" aria-label="Reader snapshot"><button type="button" data-view="fresh" aria-pressed="true">New statement snapshot</button><button type="button" data-view="fixed" aria-pressed="false">One fixed snapshot</button></div>
 <div class="views"><div role="group" aria-label="Reader view"><p class="label">Reader’s eligible rows</p><p data-reader data-live>101 · 102 · 103 · 105 · 106</p></div><div role="group" aria-label="Current committed state"><p class="label">Current committed eligible rows</p><p data-committed data-live>101 · 102 · 103 · 105 · 106</p></div></div>
 <div class="events"><p data-event data-live>Start before the writer changes anything.</p><p data-output data-live>No batch read yet.</p></div>
 <p data-explanation data-live aria-live="polite">Both views contain all five orders. Advance to the first batch.</p>
 <div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Snapshot figure controls"><button type="button" data-play>Play</button><button type="button" data-back>Back</button><button type="button" data-next>Next</button><button type="button" data-reset>Reset</button><span data-step>1 / 4</span></div><div class="hold" data-hold aria-hidden="true"><span></span></div>
 <details><summary>The complete schedule as text</summary><p>First, the reader gets 101 and 102. A separate writer then cancels 103 and commits. A new statement snapshot sees only 101, 102, 105 and 106; continuing after 102 returns 105 and 106. With one fixed snapshot established before that commit, the reader still sees all five original rows; continuing after 102 returns 103 and 105. The writer’s change committed in either case. This illustration concerns ordinary reads, without row locks.</p></details>
</figure>
<script>
 import {stepper} from '../../figures/stepper.mjs';
 document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-export-snapshot]').forEach(root=>{
 let fixed=false;
 const player=stepper(root,{steps:()=>4,draw:(step)=>{
 const changed=step>=2,readerChanged=step>=3&&!fixed;
 root.querySelector('[data-reader]')!.textContent=readerChanged?'101 · 102 · 105 · 106':'101 · 102 · 103 · 105 · 106';
 root.querySelector('[data-committed]')!.textContent=changed?'101 · 102 · 105 · 106':'101 · 102 · 103 · 105 · 106';
 root.querySelector('[data-event]')!.textContent=['Start before the writer changes anything.','Reader: fetch the first two matching orders.','Writer: cancel order 103 and commit.','Reader: continue after order 102.'][step];
 root.querySelector('[data-output]')!.textContent=step===0?'No batch read yet.':step<3?'First batch: 101, 102':fixed?'Second batch: 103, 105':'Second batch: 105, 106';
 root.querySelector('[data-explanation]')!.textContent=step===0?'Both views contain all five orders. Advance to the first batch.':step===1?'The first read establishes the snapshot. Both settings return 101 and 102.':step===2?'The writer has committed: current matching rows no longer include 103. The reader has not started its next query yet.':fixed?'The reader retains its earlier view, so 103 still belongs in this export. Its cancellation remains committed for later readers.':'The new query takes a new view. Order 103 no longer qualifies, so this traversal omits a row that belonged to the initial set.';
 }});
 root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-view]').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{fixed=b.dataset.view==='fixed';root.querySelectorAll('[data-view]').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(x===b)));player.render();}));
 });
</script>
